10252 ~     // /// This is the production wiring Stage 188D deliberately left open: 188D built the rank-3
10253 ~     // /// halves (`try_record_reply_waiter_cap_split` / `clear_reply_waiter_cap_split`) and proved
10254 ~     // /// them by unit test, but said "wiring a live producer there is out of Stage 188D scope".
10255 ~     // /// U9-C wires the producer, so the ONE live reply-cap materialization a real boot performs
10256 ~     // /// (PM receiving a call that carries a reply cap) no longer needs a broad `&mut KernelState`.
10257 ~     // ///
10258 ~     // /// # Rank order — sequential, never nested
10259 ~     // ///
10260 ~     // /// | phase | rank | effect |
10261 ~     // /// |---|---|---|
10262 ~     // /// | A | 3 (IPC) | consume the transfer envelope exactly once; read its `source_object` |
10263 ~     // /// | B | 3 (IPC) | the named `Reply{index,generation}` is still live |
10264 ~     // /// | C | 2 then 4 | resolve the receiver's process CNode |
10265 ~     // /// | D | 4 (+6 no-op) | mint the receiver-local one-shot Reply cap |
10266 ~     // /// | E | 3 (IPC) | record the exact CapId as the reply record's waiter alias |
10267 ~     // ///
10268 ~     // /// Each phase fully RELEASES its domain before the next acquires one, so the capability lock
10269 ~     // /// is never held while the IPC lock is taken. That is what removes the
10270 ~     // /// `reply_cap_ipc_rank_inversion` at this site: the inversion was never the order 4-then-3, it
10271 ~     // /// was holding 4 *while* taking 3.
10272 ~     // ///
10273 ~     // /// # Exactness
10274 ~     // ///
10275 ~     // /// Nothing here is derived from a bare TID, a bare slot or the ambient current task. The
10276 ~     // /// envelope consume is generation-guarded and endpoint-matched; the Reply object carries its
10277 ~     // /// own `{index, generation}`; the record write is generation-guarded again in phase E, which is
10278 ~     // /// what closes the mint→record window. If phase E finds the reply object revoked or reused, the
10279 ~     // /// phase-D mint is rolled all the way back (slot + refcount) and the whole transaction fails
10280 ~     // /// closed — no cap published, no alias set, no envelope resurrected.
10281 ~     // ///
10282 ~     // /// A shared-region envelope (`pinned_object.is_some()`) is NOT serviced here: it owes a rank-6
10283 ~     // /// pin release this transaction deliberately does not perform. Its arrival is a routing bug, so
